# ñ as n and y rather than n, as ETI Eloquence 6.1 reads it.
#
# normalize_eng_letters in et_norm.dr gives an accented letter English has
# no rule for the plain letter it is written on, so ñ became n: El Niño was
# nee-no and España es-pah-nuh. 6.1 says the ny of the languages ñ comes
# from. Its own normalize_eng_letters leaves accented letters to letter
# rules written for them, which ours are not, so this changes the one
# branch: ñ is replaced by the two letters n and y, which the letter rules
# read as they read the ny of canyon. It stands where the wrapper that wrote
# n stood, and has its shape. Every dictionary is asked after this, so the
# words they hold with ñ are filed under n and y as well; docs/quirks.md
# says where.

rule enye_as_ny takes 3 from et_norm.obj
  bare
  afresh
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 1 2 sym lts_enye 0
  if answer is not 0
    go to refused
  end
  answer 0
place refused
  answer 1
end

# English writes the plain letter for an accented one before any dictionary
# or letter rule sees a word, in normalize_eng_letters, and Russian's letters
# arrive as the bytes of those accented letters: left to it, every Russian
# word would be rewritten into Latin letters before it was read. So the one
# call that reaches normalize_eng_letters reaches this instead, which leaves
# the letters as they were written. Bare, since it stands where a rule of
# IBM's stood inside another rule's choice points, and what it answers is not
# asked.
rule ru_letters_as_written takes 3 from et_norm.obj
  bare
  afresh
  answer 0
end
